<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft was a German engine and car maker founded in 1890 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-maybach">Wilhelm Maybach</a> at Cannstatt near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. Its cars were sold under the name Mercedes from 1902, and in 1926 it merged with Benz &amp; Cie to form Daimler-Benz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daimler-motoren-gesellschaft.jpg" alt="Daimler-Motoren-Gesellschaft" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DMG-Werbung-1890.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-maybach">Wilhelm Maybach</a> had both left Nikolaus Otto's Deutz Gasmotorenfabrik by 1882. On 5 July 1887 Daimler bought a property on Seelberg Hill in Cannstatt, formerly a precious metal foundry, which cost 30,200 Goldmark. From it the two men produced engines for their motorboat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>, built in 1886, and sold licences for others to make their engines.</p><p>Sales were too low and the licences brought little profit, so an agreement was reached with the industrialists Max von Duttenhofer and Wilhelm Lorenz and the banker Kilian von Steiner to turn the private business into a public corporation. Some historians regard this agreement as a devil's pact, since the inventors never got along with the new status. The financiers doubted that cars would sell and expanded the stationary engine business instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Daimler and Maybach pressed for car manufacture and left the firm for a short period. Their friend Frederick Simms persuaded the financiers to take them back, under a contract of reassociation signed on 1 November 1895, and the businesses were merged in early 1896. In 1894 the Phoenix engine, with four vertical parallel cylinders and a spray-nozzle carburettor, became the company's main product until 1902. The first Daimler car appeared in 1892.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> died in 1900. Emil Jellinek, an Austrian merchant who raced cars, ordered racing cars from Maybach and promised to buy 36 of them for 550,000 Goldmark if he became sole concessionaire in several countries. The success of the Mercedes model changed the board's view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a>. A great fire destroyed the Cannstatt plant on the night of 10 June 1903, along with 93 finished cars, and production moved to Untertürkheim, which became the headquarters in 1904.</p><p>Other works were opened at Berlin-Marienfelde in 1902 for marine engines and later trucks, and at Sindelfingen in 1915 for military vehicles and aircraft engines. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> limited the Sindelfingen factory to car bodies. The firm tripled its capital in 1920 and moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">Inflation</a> and strikes in the early 1920s pushed the firm to the limit. Output fell to 1,020 units in 1923, against 1,382 for Benz &amp; Cie., and DMG made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typewriter">typewriters</a> and issued its own emergency money. The two rivals signed an Agreement of Mutual Interest in 1924, and on 28 June 1926 they merged as Daimler-Benz AG, headquartered at Untertürkheim. Their cars took the name Mercedes-Benz, with a three-pointed star inside the laurels of Karl Benz's logo. The star, which Daimler's sons proposed and the board accepted in June 1909, stood for engines on land, water and air.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daimler Motoren Gesellschaft” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In industry, the inventions of <b>Daimler</b>, Maybach and Bosch made Stuttgart one of the birthplaces of the motor car and its electrical equipment, firms that still bear their founders' names, while Zeppelin's airships, Margarete Steiff's toy animals at Giengen and Märklin's model trains at Göppingen became widely known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Württemberg had a balanced economy of small farms and skilled industry, with companies such as Bosch and <b>Daimler</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg">Free People's State of Württemberg</a></li></ul></section>
